<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">De revolutionibus orbium coelestium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1543">1543</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres, De revolutionibus, On the Revolutions</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">De revolutionibus orbium coelestium, On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres, is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>'s account of his heliocentric theory, printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1543, shortly before his death. Following the layout of Ptolemy's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a>, its six books describe an Earth that turns daily on its axis and circles the Sun with the other planets, though Copernicus kept uniform circular motions and epicycles. An unsigned preface by the Lutheran preacher Andreas Osiander presented the system as an aid to computation, not literal truth. In 1616 the Congregation of the Index suspended the book until corrected.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/de-revolutionibus-orbium-coelestium.jpg" alt="De revolutionibus orbium coelestium" width="320" height="503" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mikołaj Kopernik · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Revolvtionibvs_Orbium_coelestium_1543_(121406887)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Science spoke Latin for two more centuries: Copernicus’s <b>On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres</b> of 1543, William Harvey’s account of the circulation of the blood, Newton’s Principia of 1687 and the works in which Carl Linnaeus named plants and animals with two Latin words were all written…</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Nicolaus Copernicus, a canon of Frombork in Warmia, published <b>On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres</b>, which placed the Sun near the centre of the universe and made the Earth a planet, turning daily on its axis and circling the Sun each year; an unsigned preface by the…</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Copernicus's '<b>On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres</b>', published as he died in 1543, placed the Sun rather than the Earth at the centre.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-19">19 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In connection with the Galileo affair, the Congregation of the Index suspended <b>De revolutionibus</b> until it could be &quot;corrected&quot;, calling Copernicanism a &quot;false Pythagorean doctrine&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-5">5 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Copernicus died weeks after his <b>On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres</b> was printed; tradition says he saw a copy on his deathbed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-24">24 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
